<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kullu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kulu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kullu is a town and district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> River. Formerly called Sultanpur, the town became the capital of the Kullu kingdom in the 17th century, when Raja Jagat Singh installed the image of Raghunath there, and it hosts the annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu-dussehra">Kullu Dussehra</a> festival. The valley passed to British rule in 1846 and is now known for tourism, apples and woollen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawl">shawls</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kullu.jpg" alt="Kullu" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beas_Valley_-_Palchan_-_Kullu_2014-05-10_2275.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kullu is a municipal council town and the administrative headquarters of Kullu district. It stands on the banks of the Beas River in the Kullu Valley, about 10 km north of the airport at Bhuntar, at an average elevation of 1,278 m. The valley lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pir-panjal-range">Pir Panjal</a>, Lower Himalayan and Great Himalayan ranges, and the river, which rises at Beas Kund, passes a series of hillside settlements among forests of deodar, with pine on the lower rocky ridges. The town is 497 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The valley appears in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> literary works, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>. During the Vedic period several small republics known as janapadas existed there. They were later conquered by the Nanda, Mauryan, Shunga, Gupta, Pala and Karkoṭa powers. After a brief period of supremacy under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harshavardhana</a> the region again divided among local chieftains, including some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> principalities, which were later conquered by the Mughal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> empires.</p><p>The Buddhist pilgrim monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visited the valley in 634 or 635 CE. He described a fertile region completely surrounded by mountains, with about twenty Buddhist monasteries and about 1,000 monks, most of them following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> tradition, and some fifteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples. Both faiths used meditation caves near the mountain passes. The country was said to produce gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, crystal lenses and bell-metal. In 1650, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-singh-of-kullu">Jagat Singh of Kullu</a>, the Raghunath Temple was established after a priest, Pandit Damodar, took the idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> from the Tretanath Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> to Kullu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kullu's offices include those of the Deputy Commissioner, the Superintendent of Police and the district courts. The people speak the Kullui language. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded a literacy rate of 79.4 per cent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kullu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kullu</b> appears in early Indian texts as Kuluta, and local tradition traces its Pal dynasty to a founder called Behangamani Pal.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kullu">Kingdom of Kullu</a></li><li><blockquote>Sangri was one of the small Simla Hill States, held by a branch of the ruling family of <b>Kullu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sangri-state">Sangri State</a></li></ul></section>
